# Concurrent notes design

State: novel-work planning. Proposed design.

Both existing functions can read the same old note, modify different fields and overwrite each other's changes. Move all reads, field updates and writes into one worker queue for this file. Create the worker once during application startup and inject the same object into both clients; neither client writes JSON itself.

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path
from typing import TypedDict

class Note(TypedDict):
    id: str
    title: str
    body: str
    tags: list[str]

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TextChange:
    title: str
    body: str

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class TagsChange:
    tags: tuple[str, ...]

class NoteWriter:
    def __init__(self, path: Path): ...
    def update(self, note_id: str, change: TextChange | TagsChange) -> Note: ...
    def close(self) -> None: ...

def handle_edit(writer: NoteWriter, note_id: str, title: str, body: str) -> Note:
    return writer.update(note_id, TextChange(title, body))

def run_tagging(writer: NoteWriter, note_id: str, tags: list[str]) -> Note:
    return writer.update(note_id, TagsChange(tuple(tags)))

writer = NoteWriter(Path('data/notes.json'))
# Application startup injects this same writer into handler and scheduled job.
updated_text = handle_edit(writer, 'b7', 'Bridge east', 'Inspection complete')
try:
    updated_tags = run_tagging(writer, 'b7', ['survey', 'approved'])
except Exception as cause:
    log_failure(cause)  # log_failure(cause: Exception) -> None
```

update enqueues the ID and field-specific immutable change and blocks for its own receipt. The sole worker reads the latest JSON, changes only the requested fields, and writes the full retained record set to a temporary file beside notes.json. Flush and fsync that file, atomically replace notes.json, then fsync its directory before acknowledging success. Return a copied updated Note from that operation. The next operation reads the latest committed file, so distinct-field changes survive even when requests overlap. Do not take a caller's stale full record as replacement input. The original JSON array shape remains readable.

On a failed read or persistence operation, carry the original exception object in that request's receipt and raise it in the waiting caller; do not acknowledge success. If replacement happened before a later durability error, the change may already be visible, and the caller receives an error rather than a durability promise. A failed request does not kill the worker or reuse another request's receipt. close stops accepting requests, drains acknowledged work and joins the worker. The application owns this writer for its lifetime and closes it at shutdown. Only the application composition code constructs it; both clients receive the existing instance, and there are no other direct writers.

The queue makes unrelated writes wait for disk I/O and adds worker lifecycle code. This is acceptable for the single local file and avoids shared-lock ownership in the two clients. There is no requested cancellation operation: a queued synchronous update runs to its receipt. This is an API and responsibility decision for agreement, not production code.

